    We already showed that $n \equiv 4 \pmod 6$ implies $n \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ and $n \equiv 0 \pmod 2$.
    If $n \equiv 0 \pmod 2$ and $n \equiv 2 \pmod 4$, this is consistent ($n=4j+2$).
    If $n \equiv 4 \pmod 6$, then $n=6k+4$.
